Title: Context length audit derives its levels from ceilings.json
Created: 2026-11-10
Author: reviewer

--- log ---
2026-11-10T13:15Z reviewer created

--- body ---
## Context

`scripts/trace-compact-audit.py` reads each session's ceiling from
`skills/route/ceilings.json`, but the three rows "sessions with 30 or more
turns at or above N" come from a literal, `LEVELS = (230_000, 450_000,
550_000)`, a copy of the three values that file holds today. Change a ceiling
in `ceilings.json` and the over-ceiling count follows while those rows keep
the old values. The author ruled on 2026-11-10: "Derive numbers. Drift is
certain otherwise."

Read on main at 8326edd1:

- `LEVELS` at line 95 is the only code copy of a ceiling value under
  `scripts/`, `hooks/` and `skills/` (grep of 230000, 450000, 550000 with and
  without underscore, `ceilings.json` and tests set aside).
- The same file writes the default ceiling as text twice: "230000 for
  subagents" in the `--threshold` help and "the default (230K)" in the
  `audit_corpus` docstring.
- The run floor is written twice: `MIN_TURNS_AT_LEVEL = 30` and the
  `--min-run` default of 30.
- `tests/test_context_length_audit.py` asserts 230000, 450000 and 550000
  against the live `ceilings.json`, so a ruling that changes a ceiling turns
  those tests red for no defect.

Not in this ticket: `FLEET_HOSTS = ("padme", "doudou")` in the same script.
Whether the fleet is read from the archive, which would name the hosts by its
own directories, is an open question with the author.

## Scope

- The level rows of the table and of the JSON are the distinct ceilings of the
  ceilings data the run uses, the default included, in increasing order.
- No ceiling value is written in the script, its help or its docstrings; text
  that names one takes it from the data.
- The run floor has one definition.
- The tests of the audit's logic use ceilings of their own, so they hold when
  the author changes a ceiling; one test reads the live file to show the audit
  follows it.

Out of scope: the prose of rules and skills that states 230K to workers; the
30-turn and 100-turn floors themselves, which are this audit's own
definitions and stay.

## Test

With a ceilings fixture that differs from the live file (another default,
another model ceiling, a third value): the level rows are that fixture's
values, and a session past the fixture's ceiling and under the live one is
counted past its ceiling.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The audit's level rows come from the ceilings data: a ceiling changed in
  `skills/route/ceilings.json` changes the rows with no edit to the script
- [ ] No ceiling value is written in `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py`, code,
  help or docstring
- [ ] The run floor of 30 turns has one definition in the script
- [ ] The audit's tests do not fail when a ceiling changes in the live file
- [ ] `make check` passes
